so be having these worries  and what does that mean  i guess by way of confidence within  caucus around the government's handling  of the endemic  marco i'll i'll i'll jump in first of  all i've been a member of of the liberal  caucus now for over six years and  and and and i believe that we are a  caucus that that listens to each other  and that there's plenty of opportunity  for for a wide variety of opinion within  that caucus i think  more i reflect on you know the broader  anxiety and frustration that canadians  are feeling with respect to this  pandemic it's been very difficult for  everyone um and and i would just simply  respond that that we have all been  working full out in first of all to keep  canadians safe and to take the steps  that that our health officials have  advised and that we believe were the  right thing to do in order to to ensure  people were safe i think one of the one  one of what is clear is that vaccination  is is is a an incredibly important part  of keeping canadians safe and and and  acknowledge that the overwhelming  majority of canadians close to 90  percent have have now gone out and  become fully vaccinated we're going to  continue to work on those things and at  the same time in we recognize that as we  work through this pandemic and the  situation continues to evolve  we we are also we share all canadians  hope um that that we will be able to to  return to some some greater sense of  normalcy everyone is anxious to see that  happen we're anxious to see it happen  too and we're all working tirelessly to  make it happen and and part of that is  making sure that we provide you know  with with our health officials and and  others the supports they need access to  vaccinations and take the measures that  that will keep us safe and at the same  time when the need for those measures  begins to abate we're always evaluating  the necessity because we recognize the  impact that even our our most important  measures can have on all canadians  oh yeah i'm sorry just a follow-up um i  believe this is probably from mr mancino  one of the things that mr leibn  highlighted is is the need to pivot and  start thinking more longer term and i do  take your point and you and i know that  you said this on monday as well um that  when it comes to the protests and the  situations that have been going on here  um  you're saying that there's a need to  focus on the short-term goal of  operation so on the ground with law  enforcement but i guess my question is  just given now that we have  uh one of your one of your colleagues  now publicly striking a similar message  does that place  a sense of urgency  on focusing on these longer-term  reflections sooner rather than later and  by extension will there be some  formalization of these talks  i think we may have lost minister  mendocino uh minister blair do would you  want to respond to that  yeah if if if i may and i'm sorry that  that was that technically we seem to  have lost minister mendocino and i know  he  he would have  wanted to respond  um to your question you know  in in every circumstance we have to deal  with the immediate the immediate impact  of of the protests that are taking place  in ottawa the the  the  blockades that are are are you know uh  occasionally popping up in alberta and  and and and now in windsor and in other  places are are urgent matters that need  to be dealt with in an urgent way and at  the same time i just want to assure  everyone including  all canadians that that that we are also  you know looking very carefully at at  at the future and how we can bring the  country  um through this pandemic and and beyond  you know the the circumstances we find  ourselves in now there is a long-term  view and and all of our officials are  seized with that responsibility we  recognize that and as we have from the  very beginning we've heard from  canadians who were concerned about  restrictions on international travel  their ability to see family and friends  um you know this the the impact on on  that some of our provinces have  restrictions that they've put in place  with respect to kids going to school the  impact on the business community from  the very beginning  we knew  we needed to be there for canadians to  provide the supports financial supports  and other supports in in order to to  support canadians but also to take the  steps that were necessary to keep them  safe we we have seen that that we have  made some progress in in in this  pandemic we've learned some lessons um  the data you know our public health  officials work relentlessly to gather  data and to learn from the experience of  other jurisdictions and apply it here  and and we are also hopeful that as we  work through this some of the  restrictions that have been absolutely  critical and necessary and successful in  keeping canadians safe  that we may have the opportunity to  begin to ease those restrictions at the  appropriate time based on the evidence  based on the advice that we receive from  our health officials we're always seized  by that um and and we acknowledge the  frustration but then i'll go back to the  matter of urgency  when when people express that  frustration and everybody's entitled to  to to the to freedom of expression but  when they do it in such a way it impacts  on on the lives and the safety on the  health of other canadians  you know the threats of of violence the  intimidation the the symbols of hatred  and and intolerance that have been on  display even quite frankly some of the  delusional um  modifications of people who think that  that they are perhaps more fit to take  over the government all of all of those  things need unfortunately to be dealt  with in the immediate but we are we'll  never take our eye off the best interest  of canadians we are all uh focused and  and and and seized with that  responsibility and we're working through  it  thank you very much this ends this press  conference  thank you  you

